  "body": {
    "id": "thm:matching-full-modulus",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every fixed nondegenerate bounded class \\(\\mathcal M_{n,k,m}(c_0,C_0,K,a_0,J,\\Delta_0)\\) containing the lower family, there exist \\(0<c\\le C<\\infty\\) such that \\(c\\varepsilon^{(k+1)/(k+m+1)}\\le\\Omega_{k,m}(\\varepsilon)\\le C\\varepsilon^{(k+1)/(k+m+1)}\\) for all sufficiently small \\(\\varepsilon\\), with \\(d_{\\mathrm{rep}}\\) the symmetric aligned discrepancy. The primary class \\(\\mathcal M^{\\mathrm{Morse}}_{2,2,1}\\) is justified directly by its \\(C^2\\) Hessian condition and has exponent \\(3/4\\). At \\(\\varepsilon=0\\), the finite-type conditions imply direct componentwise circular recovery up to a common node permutation; this has the same conclusion type as the cited exact-identification results. The strengthened smoothness, conditioning, separation, and attained-order assumptions are used only for the uniform nonzero-error frontier.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "model_class",
      "n",
      "k",
      "m",
      "c0",
      "C0",
      "K",
      "a0",
      "J",
      "Delta0",
      "inverse_modulus",
      "epsilon",
      "primary_morse_class",
      "representation_distance"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:model-class",
      "def:inverse-modulus",
      "lem:full-law-residual-reduction",
      "lem:scalar-finite-type-weak-rigidity",
      "thm:localized-lower-bound",
      "prop:lower-family-membership",
      "prop:primary-morse-audit",
      "def:primary-morse-class",
      "def:representation-distance"
    ],
    "justification": "The theorem names the critical-order recovery frontier in a symmetric quotient geometry and separates the directly stated \\(C^2\\) Morse headline from the novel general finite-type rate class.",
    "gap": "Liang et al. and Varıcı et al. prove qualitative exact recovery under almost-everywhere discrepancy; no cited Morse theorem supplies the \\(k=2\\) rate assumptions, and no comparator gives a symmetric law-based sharp exponent at isolated critical points.",
    "consumer": "Likelihood and score-based causal representation learners obtain a calibrated translation from population objective error to encoder error.",
    "proof_tex": "Set\n\\[\np=\\frac{k+1}{k+m+1},\n\\qquad\nq=\\frac{k}{(k+n/2)(m+1)}.\n\\]\nTheorem \\(\\mathrm{thm:localized\\text{-}lower\\text{-}bound}\\), applied to the family whose membership is certified by Proposition \\(\\mathrm{prop:lower\\text{-}family\\text{-}membership}\\), gives constants \\(c>0\\) and \\(\\varepsilon_0>0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Omega_{k,m}(\\varepsilon)\\geq c\\varepsilon^p,\n\\qquad 0<\\varepsilon\\leq\\varepsilon_0.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nFor an arbitrary pair in the same class with \\(D_H(\\theta,\\widetilde\\theta)\\leq\\varepsilon\\), Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem:full\\text{-}law\\text{-}residual\\text{-}reduction}\\) and Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem:scalar\\text{-}finite\\text{-}type\\text{-}weak\\text{-}rigidity}\\) give, in this order,\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(\\theta,\\widetilde\\theta)\n\\leq C_0D_H(\\theta,\\widetilde\\theta)\n\\leq C_0\\varepsilon\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nd_{\\mathrm{rep}}(\\theta,\\widetilde\\theta)\n\\leq C_1\\mathcal E(\\theta,\\widetilde\\theta)^q\n\\leq C_1C_0^q\\varepsilon^q.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nTaking the supremum over the set in Definition \\(\\mathrm{def:inverse\\text{-}modulus}\\) proves the upper inequality.  Combining it with (1) proves the displayed bracket.\n\nFor \\((n,k,m)=(2,2,1)\\), direct substitution gives\n\\[\np=\\frac{3}{4},\n\\qquad\nq=\\frac{2}{(2+1)(1+1)}=\\frac13.\n\\]\nProposition \\(\\mathrm{prop:primary\\text{-}morse\\text{-}audit}\\) supplies the lower family inside the stated primary class, so the specialized bracket follows.  Finally (2) with \\(D_H=0\\) gives \\(d_{\\mathrm{rep}}=0\\) for every zero-law pair.  Taking the supremum over those pairs yields \\(\\Omega_{k,m}(0)=0\\).  By Definition \\(\\mathrm{def:representation\\text{-}distance}\\), this is precisely recovery up to the common permutation and the coordinatewise circle-diffeomorphism action.",
